Git Practice Questions (Hands-On Tasks)
Create a new repository named student-project and initialize it with Git.
Create a file named index.html and add some sample content to it.
Check the status of the repository and identify which files are untracked.
Add index.html to the Staging Area.
Commit the staged file with the message Initial Commit.
Modify index.html by adding a heading and some text.
Check the repository status again and identify which area contains the changes.
Stage the modified file and then unstage it without losing the changes.
Stage the file again and commit it with the message Updated Home Page.
Display the complete commit history of the repository.
Display the commit history in a one-line format.
Display only the last 2 commits.
View the detailed information of the latest commit.
Create a new branch named login-feature.
Switch to the login-feature branch.
Create a file named login.html and add some content to it.
Stage and commit login.html with the message Added Login Page.
Display all local branches and identify the active branch.
Switch back to the main branch.
Verify whether login.html exists on the main branch.
Merge the login-feature branch into main.
Delete the login-feature branch after the merge.
Create another branch named payment-feature.
Create a file named payment.html and commit it.
Push the payment-feature branch to GitHub for the first time.
Set upstream tracking while pushing the branch to GitHub.
Display all remote branches available in the repository.
Create three commits in a branch and then remove the latest commit while keeping changes in the Staging Area.
Remove the latest commit while keeping changes in the Working Directory.
Remove the latest commit and all associated changes completely.
Create a branch named bug-fix and make a commit in it.
Push the bug-fix branch to GitHub.
Merge the bug-fix branch into main and delete it.
Display the commit history using graph view and explain the branch structure.
Identify the command used to unstage a file from the Staging Area.
Identify the command used to compare two commits.
Identify the command used to find who modified a specific line in a file.
Explain the difference between git reset --soft, git reset --mixed, and git reset --hard.
A developer accidentally committed incorrect code. Remove the commit but keep the changes for further modification.
A production issue occurred after deployment. Identify the recent commits and inspect the exact code changes introduced by the latest commit.
Draw and explain the Git Architecture showing:
Working Directory
Staging Area
Local Repository
Remote Repository
Explain the difference between Centralized Version Control System (CVCS) and Distributed Version Control System (DVCS).
Explain why Git is considered a Distributed Version Control System.
Explain the complete flow of:
Working Directory → Staging Area → Local Repository → Remote Repository
Perform the complete workflow:
Initialize Repository → Create File → Add → Commit → Create Branch → Modify File → Commit → Push Branch → Merge Branch → Delete Branch → View Commit Logs.
